  "query_preview": "..\n\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Scattered showers and thunderstorms with gusty winds in \n   western Montana today.\n\n-  Temporary relief from smoke with easterly winds, except for\n   north-central Idaho.\n\n-  A cooler, active shower and storm pattern continues through\n   Friday before a ridge of high pressure slowly rebuilds this\n   weekend.\n\nIsolated showers and thunderstorms will continue to stream over\nportions of northwest and west-central Montana through mid-\nmorning, bringing gusty winds and occasional lightning. Afternoon\nand evening thunderstorms will return as a secondary surge of\nmoisture interacts with a Pacific trough and afternoon heating.\nAbundant lightning and sudden wind gusts exceeding 40 mph, along\nwith localized heavy rain, will be possible. This is most\nconcerning in deep canyons, across burn scars, and urban areas.\nThere is a 25 percent probability for rain rates to reach 0.25\ninches per hour under the stronger thunderstorms. Southwest\nMontana has the highest likelihood of experiencing the strongest\nthunderstorms through 10 PM.\n\nAfternoon temperatures today and Friday will trend 5 to 10 degrees\nbelow normal, with highs in the mid- to upper 70s and low 80s.\nEast to northeast winds will remain elevated, but not as strong as\nyesterday evening.\n\nA ridge of high pressure will gradually rebuild over the Northern\nRockies this weekend into early next week under southwest winds\naloft. While afternoon temperatures will warm, monsoonal moisture\ndrawn in by the southwest winds will keep atmospheric moisture\nelevated. This will sustain near-daily afternoon shower and\nthunderstorm chances through the weekend and into next week,\npreventing a return to completely dry conditions. Wildfire smoke\nwill remain a challenge through the forecast period, with little\nimprovement when the southwest flow returns this weekend, as the\nsource regions of the smoke is located in Washington and Oregon.",
